---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import Container from '../../components/Container.astro';
import SectionIntro from '../../components/SectionIntro.astro';
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro';
import PullQuote from '../../components/PullQuote.astro';
import FaqList from '../../components/FaqList.astro';
import FaqItem from '../../components/FaqItem.astro';
import SiteCta from '../../components/SiteCta.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../data/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Sacred No | She Who Remembers"
  description="A pathway for women learning cleaner boundaries, more accurate communication, and self-respect: saying no without becoming cruel, and protecting a true yes."
>
  <Hero
    heading="The Sacred No"
    lede="Reclaiming Your Energy and Voice"
    surface="light"
    spacing="compact"
  >
    <p>A boundary is not a punishment. It is information made visible.</p>
    <p>
      This doorway is for the woman whose yes has been doing too much work. Not because she is
      weak. Because she became fluent at keeping rooms comfortable, and less fluent at telling
      the truth in time.
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="This may be for you if">
        <p>Recognition is enough. You do not need a dramatic story to belong here.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <ul class="fit">
        <li>You say yes and feel the cost later, often as irritability you then apologize for.</li>
        <li>A boundary arrives as a paragraph, a justification, a small trial in your own defense.</li>
        <li>Someone else's disappointment still registers in your body as evidence that you failed.</li>
        <li>Anger shows up late, after a long season of being reasonable, and then you distrust it.</li>
        <li>Keeping the peace has become a practice that quietly leaves you out of the arrangement.</li>
        <li>You agree before you have consulted yourself, then spend days recovering from your own mouth.</li>
      </ul>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="What your anger may be protecting">
        <p>Anger can carry information. That does not make every surge of it accurate, or kind, or ready to be spoken raw.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Sometimes anger is the last messenger still willing to report a violated limit. A need
          that was treated as optional. A truth you swallowed to remain easy to love. Years of
          over-giving that were praised as character.
        </p>
        <p>
          The work is not to crown anger as a personality, or to exile it as unspiritual. It is to
          listen for what it has been trying to keep intact: time, dignity, energy, a private
          inner vote. Then to decide, with more precision than heat, what to do with that news.
        </p>
      </div>
      <PullQuote>
        Information is not the same as instruction. Anger can tell the truth without getting to
        drive.
      </PullQuote>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container>
      <SectionIntro heading="Boundary and retaliation">
        <p>These are easy to confuse when you have been silent for a long time. They are not the same act.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="split">
        <Card variant="blush">
          <h3>A boundary</h3>
          <p>
            Names what you will and will not participate in. It is about your side of the line:
            your time, your body, your attention, your home, your work. It can be quiet. It can
            be firm. It does not require the other person to suffer in order to be real.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card>
          <h3>Retaliation</h3>
          <p>
            Tries to make someone feel the cost they refused to see. It often looks like delayed
            punishment, public exposure, or a no designed to wound. It can be understandable. It
            still rearranges the mess instead of restoring you.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="What a Sacred No means">
        <p>Not a brand of toughness. A return to proportion.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A clean no uses fewer explanations. It can tolerate another person's disappointment
          without immediately rushing in to manage it. It protects the yes that is actually
          available, so your warmth stops being a leak.
        </p>
        <p>
          Self-respect here is practical: you choose, you communicate, and where a relationship
          still matters, you repair without erasing the limit you just named. The goal is not to
          win. It is to stop abandoning yourself in the name of being good.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container>
      <SectionIntro heading="What we explore">
        <p>The curriculum follows the places a woman's no usually goes missing.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <ul class="themes">
        <li>Energetic leaks and the small yeses that drain a week</li>
        <li>People-pleasing as a survival skill that outlived its usefulness</li>
        <li>Guilt, and how it impersonates morality</li>
        <li>Resentment as a delayed conversation with yourself</li>
        <li>Over-functioning dressed as love, leadership, or competence</li>
        <li>The clean no, and the clean yes it makes possible</li>
        <li>Self-trust after years of checking the room first</li>
        <li>Communication that is accurate without becoming a speech</li>
        <li>Boundaries after a long apprenticeship in accommodation</li>
      </ul>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="What this work is not">
        <p>Clarity about the edges keeps the work honest.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <ul class="not">
        <li>It is not permission to become cruel, or to confuse sharpness with sovereignty.</li>
        <li>It is not automatic estrangement, and it does not treat cutting people off as a spiritual credential.</li>
        <li>It is not a method for manipulating someone into treating you better.</li>
        <li>It is not therapy, diagnosis, or a substitute for mental health care.</li>
        <li>It is not legal advice, safety planning, or counsel about leaving or staying.</li>
        <li>It does not instruct you to end a relationship. That choice, if it comes, remains yours.</li>
      </ul>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="How this work is offered">
        <p>The form can change. The spine does not.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The Sacred No may appear as a focused workshop, a short course, or private integration
          support. Dates, duration, and enrollment details are shared when an offering is active.
          If nothing is open right now, a fit conversation is the honest next step.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="Questions">
        <p>Direct answers. No performance of mystery.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <FaqList>
        <FaqItem question="Will I have to confront someone?">
          <p>
            No. Confrontation is not a requirement. The work may help you notice a conversation
            or limit that matters. Sometimes the first boundary is internal: knowing what you
            actually want, delaying an automatic yes, or changing what you participate in.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="What if I feel guilty afterward?">
          <p>
            Guilt can appear when a familiar pattern changes. Feeling guilty does not
            automatically mean the boundary was wrong. The work includes learning to distinguish
            genuine responsibility from the discomfort of disappointing someone.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="Is anger required?">
          <p>
            No. Some women arrive angry, others exhausted, numb, resentful, confused, or simply
            done. Anger can be one source of information. It is not an entry requirement, and it
            is not a goal.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="What if I am in a difficult relationship?">
          <p>
            The work can support clarity about your limits, communication, and choices. It does
            not tell you whether to stay or leave. If there is coercion, violence, abuse, or
            immediate safety risk, appropriate professional or local support is needed beyond
            the scope of this work.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="Is this therapy?">
          <p>
            No. The Sacred No is coaching and educational work focused on awareness, boundaries,
            communication, and self-trust. It is not psychotherapy, diagnosis, crisis care, or a
            substitute for mental health treatment.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
      </FaqList>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <SiteCta
    heading="When you are ready for a cleaner no"
    primary={{
      label: 'Book a Fit Conversation',
      href: withBase('/book-a-conversation'),
    }}
    secondary={{ label: 'Begin Here', href: withBase('/begin-here') }}
  >
    <p>
      There is no open Sacred No enrollment on this page. A fit conversation is how we locate
      whether this doorway, another pathway, or a different kind of support is the honest next
      step.
    </p>
  </SiteCta>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .fit,
  .themes,
  .not {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .fit li,
  .themes li,
  .not li {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
    padding-left: 1.15rem;
  }

  .fit li:last-child,
  .themes li:last-child,
  .not li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .fit li::before,
  .themes li::before,
  .not li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.7em;
    width: 0.4rem;
    height: 0.4rem;
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border-accent);
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  .split {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .split h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }

    .themes {
      columns: 2;
      column-gap: var(--space-xl);
    }

    .themes li {
      break-inside: avoid;
    }
  }
</style>
